# Docker reads .dockerignore from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT. This file previously
# lived at docker/.dockerignore, where Docker never looks, so it was inert:
# every build shipped a ~735 MB context and `COPY . .` overwrote the image's
# correctly-installed Linux dependencies with the host's (Windows, pnpm
# symlink) node_modules tree, plus .env, .git and a stale .next.
#
# Keep in sync with the COPY steps in docker/*.Dockerfile.

# Dependencies — installed inside the image, never copied in.
**/node_modules

# Build output — regenerated in the builder stage.
**/.next
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# VCS + CI + agent harness
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.claude

# Secrets. Never in an image layer -- AT ANY DEPTH. Docker matches these
# against paths from the context root, so the old `.env` / `.env.*` excluded
# only the root's own: an apps/web/.env or .env.production on the build machine
# entered the context, `next build` loaded it (inlining any NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# value), and Next's standalone writer copied it into the runtime image.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example

# Local/runtime state and docs that no build step reads.
workspace
specs
docs
tests
.docker-volumes
coverage
.nyc_output
*.log
README*.md
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
Makefile

# NOTE: deliberately NOT ignored ->
#   packages/db/src/migrations/**  (the migrate image needs the .sql files and
#                                   meta/_journal.json as real files on disk)
#   apps/web/public/**             (served by the standalone server)
#   docker/**                      (Caddyfile is bind-mounted, but keep the dir)
